lisa-design:
- The lint job of ci-go-service.yml and ci-go-lib.yml is a byte-for-byte copy of ci-go-plugin.yml's (TestSharedCopies pins it), not a nested reusable workflow: keeps T03a's convention and one check name shape; a shared ci-go-lint.yml would have meant editing T03a's file.
- The required job is identical in all seven kind workflows (TestRequired), and TestRequiredNeedsEveryJob checks it needs every other job with if: always().
- ci-go-service: extra make targets run in their own `make` job (api's test-integration is slow and runs in parallel with go test -race); the job is unconditional and no-ops with no targets, because `required` treats a skipped job as a failure.
- ci-go-service: `prepare-command` (not "pre-test-command") runs before go vet/test/build and the make targets, since go vet also compiles the tests; it is not run in the lint job, which must stay identical across Go kinds (api commits its swag docs, so lint compiles without it).
- ci-ui: ESLint runs through the repo's `npm run lint` (ui's script is `eslint src/`; a bare `eslint .` would lint more than the repo intends); ESLint already fails only on errors. `test-script` input exists because ui names its Vitest script test:unit, not test.
- ci-ui: Node comes from .nvmrc when present by passing setup-node either node-version-file or node-version (the other empty), chosen in a step.
- ci-helm: `ct lint --all` instead of changed-charts-only: deterministic on pushes and needs no git history (verified in ct v3.14.0 source: merge base is computed only for change detection, upgrade and version-increment checks). Maintainer validation is left to the repo's ct.yaml.
- ci-helm: kubeconform is built from source at a pinned version with go install (sumdb-verified, like actionlint in ci-common), with -strict and no -ignore-missing-schemas (CCF charts render only core kinds).
- ci-action: actionlint is not repeated; ci-common.yml's actionlint job covers the spec row. hadolint is the release binary pinned by version and sha256 (from the release asset digest), not hadolint-action (which builds a Docker image each run).
- ci-action: hadolint lints every tracked Dockerfile* (git ls-files, so build outputs and .dockerignore are not picked up); severity threshold comes from the repo's .hadolint.yaml.
